= Hardware and System Diagram

#figure(
  image("figures/block_diagram.png", width: 120%),
  caption: [Block diagram of the flashlight system, showing power and control subsystems.],
) <figuren>

This block diagram outlines the overall flashlight system, including power input, sensing, and control subsystems. For the final project, the primary focus is on the Boost Converter block, shown as the section responsible for stepping up the voltage and providing constant-current regulation to drive the Cree XHP50.3 LED. The converter senses output current through a shunt resistor and feeds this information back to maintain stable LED brightness across varying input voltages.

This design is an initial draft architecture, where only the boost stage will be developed, tested, and characterized within the current project scope. Future integration with the STM32-based logic, sensors, and battery management circuitry will come later. A key part of this phase is evaluating whether GaN FETs or traditional silicon MOSFETs offer better performance in terms of thermal handling, switching frequency, and overall circuit footprint for the synchronous boost topology.


== Initial Boost Design with Feedback

#figure(
  image("figures/boost.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Schematic of the synchronous boost converter.],
) <figuren>

#figure(
  image("figures/current_sense.png", width: 100%),
  caption: [Schematic of the current sensing and feedback network.],
) <figuren>

This initial design uses a version of the boost converter in Lab 2, a high-current, 
non-synchronous boost converter, as the main power stage that steps the input
voltage (3–4.3 V) up above the 5.6 V (forward-voltage) required by the Cree XHP50.3 LED. 
However unlike the lab, the conververter operates in current-mode control. The LED 
current is sensed through a high-side shunt resistor, whose voltage drop represents 
the real-time LED current. The high-side placement of the shunt allows for accurate current measurement
without interfering with the LED ground reference. 

That sense voltage feeds into a dual op-amp circuit configured as a differential 
amplifier and comparator. The amplifier conditions the shunt voltage and compares 
it against a reference voltage generated by a potentiometer. By adjusting 
the potentiometer, the reference voltage changes, effectively controlling 
the output current setpoint. The op-amp’s output is routed through a variable duty 
cycle control circuitry where the duty cyvle can range from 0.5 to 0.85 to maintain 
the desired LED current.

In summary, the system forms a closed-loop constant-current boost converter, where 
the boost stage supplies the main power conversion, and the op-amp plus digital
potentiometer network provides adjustable current regulation.


== GaN Vs Tradional FETs

GaN lets you push ~3× higher switching frequency, which cuts L or output cap size by 
~3× and lowers total FET heat at this power. Silicon at lower fSW pays more in switching 
and gate-drive loss, so even with similar RDS(on), total FET loss is higher.
Although not a fair comparison the table below shows how a QFN GaN Chip (EPC2302) 
stacks up against a larger, older Si FET (IRF540N) in this application. 
