= Project Scope and Management

For this project, I will focus on designing, building, and testing the boost converter 
stage with constant-current feedback to drive the Cree XHP50.3 LED. I will also design
two other supplies to explore the use of alternate power converters. The first is the 
same boost topology however with GaN FETts to compare performance and thermal 
characteristics against the traditional silicon FET design. The second is a
small switched-capacitor voltage doubler to create high-voltage pulses for the
LED, building on the switched-capacitor concepts from Lab 3. 

In the first phase, I’ll build a basic prototype using the Totem Board 
from Lab 4 and a 250 kHz switching circuit with current feedback,
implemented entirely in analog. This setup will build directly on the work from 
Lab 3, where I developed and tested the fundamental switching and sensing stages. 
The goal is to verify that the constant-current feedback loop operates as expected 
before integrating the full boost converter. Once the analog prototype is stable,
I’ll measure temperature rise and efficiency across different loads and input 
voltages to guide the final design.

In the second phase, I’ll build a GaN-based version of the boost converter to compare
performance. Using GaN FETs should allow higher switching frequencies, reducing inductor/capacitor
size and improving thermal performance. I’ll follow a similar testing procedure to 
evaluate efficiency and heat dissipation.

Finally, I’ll design and test a switched-capacitor voltage doubler to create short,
high-voltage pulses for the LED. This stage will explore how pulsed operation 
affects brightness and thermal characteristics, building on the switched-capacitor
concepts from Lab 3.

